.TH PMC 3
.SH NAME
pmc \- performance monitoring counters
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B bind -a '#ε' /dev

.B #ε/ctrdesc
.B #ε/core
.B #ε/core0000/ctr00
.B #ε/core0000/ctr00ctl
.B #ε/core0000/ctr01
.B #ε/core0000/ctr01ctl
 ...
.B #ε/core0001/ctr00
.B #ε/core0001/ctr00ctl
 ...
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.I pmc
device serves a two-level directory giving access to the hardware
performance counters of the processors.
There is a directory per processor,
.BI core NNNN
(its number, as in
.IR getcore (2)),
for time-sharing and application cores (see
.IR execac (2))
alike.
Reading
.B core
gives the number of the processor the reader runs on, in decimal
(on an application core: that core, though its system calls run on a
time-sharing core).
.PP
Reading a
.BI ctr NN
file gives the value of the counter, in hexadecimal;
writing a number sets it.
The corresponding
.BI ctr NN ctl
file configures the counter. Reading it gives
.L enable
or
.LR disable ,
then
.L user
and
.L os
if the counter counts in user mode or in the kernel,
and its configuration.
It accepts one of these commands per write:
.TP
.B reset
Clears the configuration and the counter: it is disabled and
counts nothing.
.TP
.B user
The counter counts while the processor is in user mode.
.TP
.B os
The counter counts while the processor is in the kernel.
.TP
.BI "set " configuration
Sets what the counter counts.
The configuration is either one of the names
.B ctrdesc
lists, one per line (they depend on the processor), or numbers:
.I event
and
.I "unit mask"
on amd64 (for AMD the 12-bit event number of its event select
registers, for Intel that of its architectural events),
the event number on arm64.
.TP
.B enable
Starts the counter.
While a counter of a processor is enabled, user mode may also read
that processor's counters with the
.B RDPMC
instruction (amd64) or from
.B PMEVCNTR\fIn\fP_EL0
(arm64).
.TP
.B disable
Stops it.
.PP
.B Reset
followed by
.B user
counts in user mode only, and so on; both
.B user
and
.B os
count everywhere.
The counters count what the processor does, whatever process runs;
the counters of an application core count the one process there
(in the kernel: its idle loop).
.PP
The counters are registers of each processor. A request for the
counters of another processor is sent there with an interrupt of
its own, which an application core serves without disturbing the
process running on it; the request is served at once, not at the
next entry into the kernel. A processor that does not answer within
a second makes it fail with
.LR "connection timed out" .
.PP
Configure counter 0 of core 1 to count instructions in user mode:
.IP
.EX
% bind -a '#ε' /dev
% cat /dev/ctrdesc
cycles
instructions
 ...
% echo reset > /dev/core0001/ctr00ctl
% echo user > /dev/core0001/ctr00ctl
% echo set instructions > /dev/core0001/ctr00ctl
% echo enable > /dev/core0001/ctr00ctl
% cat /dev/core0001/ctr00
0x3e5
.EE
.SH SOURCE
.B /sys/src/9/port/devpmc.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/pc64/pmcio.c
.br
.B /sys/src/9/arm64/pmcio.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.IR pmc (2)
.SH BUGS
amd64: AMD's counters (6 with the core extension, else 4) and Intel's
architectural ones; not Intel's fixed counters. A virtual machine may
have none (QEMU with KVM: only with
.LR pmu=on ;
then the directories are empty).
arm64: the PMUv3's event counters, 32 bits wide; the cycle counter
stays the kernel's.
.PP
From NIX, which counted on AMD K8 only. 386 has no
.IR pmc .
